## Access Control Lists (ACLs)

### What is an ACL?
Set of rules that permit or deny traffic based on criteria.

### Benefits
- Security (filter traffic)
- Reduce network traffic
- Control flow
- Manage bandwidth
- Simple firewall

### ACL Types

#### Standard ACL
- Filters based on source IP only
- Range: 1-99, 1300-1999
- Matches up to source address

#### Extended ACL
- Filters on source IP, destination IP, protocol, port
- Range: 100-199, 2000-2699
- More granular control

#### Named ACL
- Standard or extended
- Named instead of numbered
- Better readability

### ACL Logic

1. ACL statements processed top-to-bottom
2. First match wins
3. Implicit deny at end (denies all unmatched traffic)
4. Order matters - most specific rules first

### Standard ACL Configuration

```
Router(config)# access-list 1 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255
Router(config)# access-list 1 deny 192.168.1.50 0.0.0.0
Router(config)# access-list 1 permit any
Router(config)# interface g0/1
Router(config-if)# ip access-group 1 out
```

Wildcard mask: Inverse of subnet mask
- 0 = must match
- 1 = don't care

### Extended ACL Configuration

```
Router(config)# access-list 100 permit tcp 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 eq 80
Router(config)# access-list 100 permit tcp 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 eq 443
Router(config)# access-list 100 deny ip 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.3.0 0.0.0.255
Router(config)# interface g0/1
Router(config-if)# ip access-group 100 in
```

### Named ACL Configuration

```
Router(config)# ip access-list standard BLOCK_SALES
Router(config-std-nacl)# deny 192.168.10.0 0.0.0.255
Router(config-std-nacl)# permit any
Router(config-std-nacl)# exit
Router(config)# interface g0/1
Router(config-if)# ip access-group BLOCK_SALES in
```

### ACL Application

- **Inbound (in):** Filters entering interface
- **Outbound (out):** Filters leaving interface
- Best practice: Apply close to destination for deny, close to source for permit

