## Network Address Translation (NAT)

### What is NAT?
Translates private IP addresses to public addresses and vice versa.

### Types of NAT

#### Static NAT
- One-to-one mapping
- Private IP always maps to same public IP
- Good for servers

**Configuration:**
```
Router(config)# ip nat inside source static 192.168.1.100 203.0.113.5
Router(config)# interface g0/1
Router(config-if)# ip nat inside
Router(config-if)# exit
Router(config)# interface g0/0
Router(config-if)# ip nat outside
```

#### Dynamic NAT
- Private IPs mapped to pool of public IPs
- First-come, first-served basis
- Uses ACL to specify which traffic gets translated

**Configuration:**
```
Router(config)# access-list 1 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255
Router(config)# ip nat pool POOL 203.0.113.1 203.0.113.254 netmask 255.255.255.0
Router(config)# ip nat inside source list 1 pool POOL
Router(config)# interface g0/1
Router(config-if)# ip nat inside
Router(config-if)# exit
Router(config)# interface g0/0
Router(config-if)# ip nat outside
```

#### Port Address Translation (PAT / NAT Overload)
- Multiple private IPs share single public IP
- Differentiates by port numbers
- Most common for home/small business

**Configuration:**
```
Router(config)# access-list 1 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255
Router(config)# ip nat inside source list 1 interface g0/0 overload
Router(config)# interface g0/1
Router(config-if)# ip nat inside
Router(config-if)# exit
Router(config)# interface g0/0
Router(config-if)# ip nat outside
```

### NAT Terminology

| Term | Definition |
|------|-----------|
| Inside Local | Private IP address |
| Inside Global | Public IP address (from inside perspective) |
| Outside Local | IP address of external device (from inside perspective) |
| Outside Global | Public IP address of external device |

